									 Deploy your Astro Site with Deno 







 You can deploy a static or on-demand rendered Astro site using Deno, either on your own server, or to  Deno Deploy , a distributed system that runs JavaScript, TypeScript, and WebAssembly at the edge, worldwide. 
 This guide includes instructions for running your Astro site on your own server with Deno, and deploying to Deno Deploy through GitHub Actions or the Deno Deploy CLI. 
 Before you begin, you need the following: 
 This guide assumes you already have  Deno  installed. 
  Project Configuration         Section titled “Project Configuration”   
 Your Astro project can be deployed as a static site, or as an on-demand rendered site. 
  Static Site         Section titled “Static Site”   
 Your Astro project is a static site by default. You don’t need any extra configuration to deploy a static Astro site with Deno, or to Deno Deploy. 
  Adapter for on-demand rendering         Section titled “Adapter for on-demand rendering”   
 To enable on-demand rendering in your Astro project using Deno, and to deploy on Deno Deploy: 
 Complete the following steps in order. 
 First, Install  the  @deno/astro-adapter  adapter  to your project’s dependencies using your preferred package manager: 
 Choose one of the following options. These are alternatives — use exactly one, not all of them. 
 If you are using npm: 
         Terminal window       npm     install     @deno/astro-adapter               
 If you are using pnpm: 
      Terminal window       pnpm     install     @deno/astro-adapter               
 If you are using yarn: 
      Terminal window       yarn     add     @deno/astro-adapter               
 Second, Update your  astro.config.mjs  project configuration file with the changes below. 
    astro.config.mjs       import   { defineConfig }   from     '  astro/config  '  ;      import   deno   from     '  @deno/astro-adapter  '  ;     
     export     default     defineConfig  ({            output:   '  server  '  ,            adapter:   deno  (),      });               
 Finally, Update your  preview  script in  package.json  with the change below. 
    package.json       {          "scripts"  : {            "dev"  :   "  astro dev  "  ,            "start"  :   "  astro dev  "  ,            "build"  :   "  astro build  "  ,            "preview"  :   "  astro preview  "            "  preview  "  :     "  deno run --allow-net --allow-read --allow-env ./dist/server/entry.mjs  "            }      }               
 You can now use this command to preview your production Astro site locally with Deno. 
 Choose one of the following options. These are alternatives — use exactly one, not all of them. 
 If you are using npm: 
      Terminal window       npm     run     preview               
 If you are using pnpm: 
      Terminal window       pnpm     run     preview               
 If you are using yarn: 
      Terminal window       yarn     run     preview               
  How to deploy         Section titled “How to deploy”   
 You can run your Astro site on your own server, or deploy to Deno Deploy through GitHub Actions or using Deno Deploy’s CLI (command line interface). 
  On your own server         Section titled “On your own server”   
 Complete the following steps in order. 
 First, Copy your project onto your server. 
 Second, Install the project dependencies using your preferred package manager: 
 Choose one of the following options. These are alternatives — use exactly one, not all of them. 
 If you are using npm: 
      Terminal window       npm     install               
 If you are using pnpm: 
      Terminal window       pnpm     install               
 If you are using yarn: 
      Terminal window       yarn     install               
 Third, Build your Astro site with your preferred package manager: 
 Choose one of the following options. These are alternatives — use exactly one, not all of them. 
 If you are using npm: 
      Terminal window       npm     run     build               
 If you are using pnpm: 
      Terminal window       pnpm     run     build               
 If you are using yarn: 
      Terminal window       yarn     run     build               
 Finally, Start your application with the following command: 
 Choose one of the following options. These are alternatives — use exactly one, not all of them. 
 If you are using static: 
      Terminal window       deno     run     -A     jsr:@std/http/file-server     dist               
 If you are using ssr: 
      Terminal window       deno     run     -A     ./dist/server/entry.mjs               
  GitHub Actions Deployment         Section titled “GitHub Actions Deployment”   
 If your project is stored on GitHub, the  Deno Deploy website  will guide you through setting up GitHub Actions to deploy your Astro site. 
 Complete the following steps in order. 
 First, Push your code to a public or private GitHub repository. 
 Second, Sign in on  Deno Deploy  with your GitHub account, and click on  New Project . 
 Third, Select your repository, the branch you want to deploy from, and select  GitHub Action  mode. (Your Astro site requires a build step, and cannot use Automatic mode.) 
 Fourth, In your Astro project, create a new file at  .github/workflows/deploy.yml  and paste in the YAML below. This is similar to the YAML given by Deno Deploy, with the additional steps needed for your Astro site. 
 Choose one of the following options. These are alternatives — use exactly one, not all of them. 
 If you are using static: 
    .github/workflows/deploy.yml       name  :   Deploy      on  : [  push  ]     
     jobs  :          deploy  :            name  :   Deploy            runs-on  :   ubuntu-latest            permissions  :              id-token  :   write     # Needed for auth with Deno Deploy              contents  :   read     # Needed to clone the repository     
           steps  :                -   name  :   Clone repository                uses  :   actions/checkout@v7     
             # Not using npm? Change `npm ci` to `pnpm i` or `yarn install`                -   name  :   Install dependencies                run  :   npm ci     
             # Not using npm? Change `npm run build` to `pnpm run build` or `yarn run build`                -   name  :   Build Astro                run  :   npm run build     
               -   name  :   Upload to Deno Deploy                uses  :   denoland/deployctl@v1                with  :                  project  :   my-deno-project     # TODO: replace with Deno Deploy project name                  entrypoint  :   jsr:@std/http/file-server                  root  :   dist               
 If you are using ssr: 
    .github/workflows/deploy.yml       name  :   Deploy      on  : [  push  ]     
     jobs  :          deploy  :            name  :   Deploy            runs-on  :   ubuntu-latest            permissions  :              id-token  :   write     # Needed for auth with Deno Deploy              contents  :   read     # Needed to clone the repository     
           steps  :                -   name  :   Clone repository                uses  :   actions/checkout@v7     
             # Not using npm? Change `npm ci` to `pnpm i` or `yarn install`                -   name  :   Install dependencies                run  :   npm ci     
             # Not using npm? Change `npm run build` to `pnpm run build` or `yarn run build`                -   name  :   Build Astro                run  :   npm run build     
               -   name  :   Upload to Deno Deploy                uses  :   denoland/deployctl@v1                with  :                  project  :   my-deno-project     # TODO: replace with Deno Deploy project name                  entrypoint  :   dist/server/entry.mjs               
 Finally, After committing this YAML file, and pushing to GitHub on your configured deploy branch, the deploy should begin automatically! 
 You can track the progress using the “Actions” tab on your GitHub repository page, or on  Deno Deploy . 
  CLI Deployment         Section titled “CLI Deployment”   
 Complete the following steps in order. 
 First, Install the  Deno Deploy CLI . 
      Terminal window       deno     install     -gArf     jsr:@deno/deployctl               
 Second, Build your Astro site with your preferred package manager: 
 Choose one of the following options. These are alternatives — use exactly one, not all of them. 
 If you are using npm: 
      Terminal window       npm     run     build               
 If you are using pnpm: 
      Terminal window       pnpm     run     build               
 If you are using yarn: 
      Terminal window       yarn     run     build               
 Third, Run  deployctl  to deploy! 
 Choose one of the following options. These are alternatives — use exactly one, not all of them. 
 If you are using static: 
      Terminal window       cd     dist   &&   deployctl     deploy     jsr:@std/http/file-server               
 If you are using ssr: 
      Terminal window       deployctl     deploy     ./dist/server/entry.mjs               
 You can track all your deploys on  Deno Deploy . 
 Finally, (Optional) To simplify the build and deploy into one command, add a  deploy-deno  script in  package.json , adapting it for your preferred package manager. 
 Choose one of the following options. These are alternatives — use exactly one, not all of them. 
 If you are using static: 
    package.json       {          "scripts"  : {            "dev"  :   "  astro dev  "  ,            "start"  :   "  astro dev  "  ,            "build"  :   "  astro build  "  ,            "preview"  :   "  astro preview  "  ,            "deno-deploy"  :   "  npm run build && cd dist && deployctl deploy jsr:@std/http/file-server  "            }      }               
 If you are using ssr: 
    package.json       {          "scripts"  : {            "dev"  :   "  astro dev  "  ,            "start"  :   "  astro dev  "  ,            "build"  :   "  astro build  "  ,            "preview"  :   "  deno run --allow-net --allow-read --allow-env ./dist/server/entry.mjs  "  ,            "deno-deploy"  :   "  npm run build && deployctl deploy ./dist/server/entry.mjs  "            }      }               
 Then you can use this command to build and deploy your Astro site in one step. 
 Choose one of the following options. These are alternatives — use exactly one, not all of them. 
 If you are using npm: 
      Terminal window       npm     run     deno-deploy               
 If you are using pnpm: 
      Terminal window       pnpm     run     deno-deploy               
 If you are using yarn: 
      Terminal window       yarn     run     deno-deploy               





